<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Ratzeburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1648 CE – 1918 CE · capital Schönberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Secularised bishopric east of Lübeck that became a detached province of Mecklenburg-Strelitz for more than two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratzeburg">Ratzeburg</a>, founded in the 12th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, had become Lutheran in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 it was secularised and given to the dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-ratzeburg">Principality of Ratzeburg</a>, though the town of Ratzeburg itself belonged to Saxe-Lauenburg.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was a rural area of villages and farms governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schonberg-mecklenburg">Schönberg</a>. It had no estates of its own, so its peasants were largely free of the noble domination typical of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a>. Under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Hamburg</a> in 1701 it was assigned to the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</a>, from which it was separated by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg-Schwerin</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratzeburg">Ratzeburg</a> remained part of Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>, raised to a grand duchy in 1815, until the monarchy ended in 1918, then belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Free State of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</a>. Today the area forms part of the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordwestmecklenburg">Nordwestmecklenburg</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-ratzeburg.jpg" alt="Principality of Ratzeburg" width="320" height="235"><figcaption>Image: de:Benutzer:Störfix · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte-Mecklenburg.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1648 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Schönberg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adolf Frederick II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fürstentum Ratzeburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1783&amp;focus=principality-of-ratzeburg" aria-label="Principality of Ratzeburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4988 473 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Ratzeburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5308 729L5308 729M5308 729L5291 598"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5308 729h0M5291 598h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5308 729h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5308 729h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Schönberg. <a href="/atlas?year=1783&amp;focus=principality-of-ratzeburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
